{
  "text": "The tutors language model employs a decoder only transformer structure to process text. A tokenizer divides an input passage into tokens, which are then mapped by an embedding layer into numerical vectors. Positional information helps the transformer distinguish sequences containing the same tokens in different orders. Self attention combines information from token representations within the attention layer. A causal attention mask prevents the model from attending to later positions when predicting the next token, a restriction that applies during both training and generation. Attention can connect information across a passage, though models with a finite context window limit the amount of text available in a single request. Attention weights support evidence for the truth of associated claims.",
  "reason": "The reconstruction follows the source occurrence order of relations provided in the data. Each relation is mapped to its corresponding concepts and definitions without inferring external facts. The causal attention mask's condition ('when the model predicts the next token') and modality ('does not' for supporting evidence) are preserved explicitly. Participant roles (e.g., tokenizer dividing input, embedding layer mapping tokens) and ownership of functions (e.g., self attention combining information) are maintained as per the supplied structured concepts."
